    <note x="10" y="1970" width="1195" height="55" fontSize="12" markdown="false" textColorLight="-16777216" textColorDark="-1" backgroundLight="0" backgroundDark="0">Spring positions are synthetic geography, not measured locations. Geometry is fixed after Setup.
Connected feasible partners are ranked by grid distance, then fee rate; remaining ties are random. Commissions are quoted at booking and paid at settlement.</note>
  </widgets>
  <info><![CDATA[## V2: SPATIAL LIQUIDITY AND COMMISSIONS

V1 (MobileBankMarkets_v1.nlogox) is preserved unchanged as the abstract model.
V2 adds external rebalancing, unequal demand, travel-related closure, spring/grid
geography, supplier commissions and an optional external commission subsidy.
This is an uncalibrated mechanism model, not a numerical policy forecast.

## SYNTHETIC GEOGRAPHY AND GRID DISTANCE

At Setup, layout-spring runs for 200 iterations using every relationship link,
after a circular initialization, then centers and uniformly scales the result
to leave a two-patch display margin. Spring strength 0.2, length 4 and repulsion 1
are fixed layout assumptions. A separate seeded stream isolates layout from
financial policy, demand and commission draws. Positions remain fixed during Go.
Tightly linked groups tend to cluster; isolates and disconnected components remain.
These positions are NOT measured geography or estimated travel distances.

grid-cell-size is a whole number of patch widths (default 5, range 1–33).
Cells are anchored at the southwest world boundary (-16.5, -16.5), with boundaries
included in the cell to their north/east. The checkerboard and lines show them.
Distance is Manhattan distance between cell indices: abs(dx) + abs(dy).
Same cell = 0 ticks, one horizontal/vertical cell = 1, one diagonal cell = 2.
There is no wrapping. Size 33 puts the whole world in one cell.

A peer trade's entire booking-to-settlement duration is this distance. It settles
immediately at distance zero. The old v2 settlement-delay and peer-travel-ticks
controls are removed. V1 retains its own fixed 0–2 tick settlement control.
Grid time is an abstract round-trip convention, not a calibrated walking speed;
there is no additional return-trip multiplier. A tick is one customer-demand batch.

Changing grid size keeps coordinates fixed but changes the partition and times.
Changing topology can change spring positions, so comparisons between networks
jointly change connectivity and assumed geography. Compare commission/subsidy
policies on the same graph, seed and grid size to hold geography fixed.

## PARTNER SELECTION

At the rebalancing phase, an uncommitted participating kiosk with a resource below
low-balance-percent tries to refill toward target-balance-percent. Both percentages
use current booked cash + e-float. Network sharing permits connected participants;
All-to-all sharing relaxes the tie requirement. No sharing disables peer swaps.

First exclude unavailable suppliers and trades that cannot fund a positive refill
while protecting outgoing minimum-reserve and requester-paid fees. Partial refills
remain valid: successful does not require reaching the full target. Rank remaining
suppliers by grid distance, then commission rate; choose randomly among exact ties
across equally near cells. Distance precedes price even when a farther agent is
cheaper. Under Uniform commissions, all equally near feasible suppliers tie.

Balance knowledge is perfect. Neither search nor strategic bargaining is modeled.
There is at most one pending exchange per kiosk and one new booking per kiosk per
tick. Instant trades still count toward that limit. Peer suppliers remain open;
the requester closes during travel when travel-closes-shop? is true. With it false,
staff can handle the trip while unreserved resources continue serving customers.

## COMMISSIONS, QUOTES AND SUBSIDY

Each supplier charges a percentage of gross liquidity exchanged, in cash:
Uniform uses commission-percent for all agents (default 1%). Varied draws one
fixed rate per agent from Uniform[commission-min-percent, commission-max-percent)
(defaults 0.5–1.5%). Equal bounds give one rate. All rates must be >=0 and <100%.
The distribution is a scenario assumption, not estimated from survey responses.
Rates use a separate seeded stream and do not change with grid size or subsidy.

commission-subsidy-percent (0–100, default 0) is the share paid by an external
sponsor. The requester pays the remainder; the supplier receives the full fee.
For gross amount q, fee = supplier-rate * q. A cash buyer pays its share from
incoming cash, receiving q minus that fee. Its desired gross refill accounts for
this deduction. An e-float buyer pays q plus its own fee in cash, so both are
included in the cash budget and reserved before departure. The supplier's gross
outgoing resource is also reserved. Incoming funds are unavailable until settlement.

Amount, fee and subsidy are fixed at booking and paid exactly once at settlement.
Supplier income = requester payments + sponsor spending. Pending fees/subsidies
are commitments, not already spent. There is no subsidy budget cap in this model.
Fees redistribute capital between kiosks. Subsidies inject cash into the market;
cash accounting explicitly includes subsidy-spent. Therefore the subsidy effect
includes both relief of requester costs and additional market working capital.
No fees apply to the separate external rebalancing provider in this increment.

## EXTERNAL REBALANCING

Disabled: only peer policy operates. External only: peers are disabled. Peers then
external: book a feasible peer refill first; otherwise try the external service.
A partial peer refill does not also trigger an external order in that tick.
The rule does not compare the external service's delay/price with a feasible peer.

External access is a fixed random assignment independent of peer participation.
The provider has ample cash/e-float, but each kiosk must exchange an equal value
of its opposite resource while keeping minimum-reserve. It supplies no loan or
grant through rebalancing. external-max-amount caps one swap. Opening condition:
(ticks mod external-cycle-ticks) < external-open-ticks. Booked orders settle even
after closing. Opening ticks cannot exceed the positive cycle length; zero means
always closed and equal open/cycle ticks means always open. There is no shared
capacity limit or endogenous queue. External completion/closure duration remains
external-travel-ticks + external-service-delay, independent of grid positions.

## CUSTOMER DEMAND AND EXACT TICK ORDER

Cash and e-float are separate resources. Deposits increase cash and spend e-float;
withdrawals do the reverse. The fixed requests-per-tick total is MARKET demand.
demand-heterogeneity controls lognormal kiosk traffic weights; size-heterogeneity
controls independent lognormal size multipliers. Zero gives uniform traffic or
equal size distributions. Size multipliers are traffic-weight normalized so expected
market transaction size remains the base uniform 1..maximum-request expectation.
Individual requests can exceed maximum-request when size heterogeneity is positive.
withdrawal-share plus/minus demand-polarization sets alternating kiosk tendencies,
clipped to [0,1]; it is not a guaranteed aggregate share with unequal traffic.

Profiles are fixed for a run. Original requests are independent of trading policy,
subsidy, grid size and commissions, holding demand settings, population and seed
fixed. There are no correlated shocks, customer retries, rerouting or households.
Customer requests are all-or-nothing. A closed shop loses the request before a
balance check; other failures are classified as cash shortage or e-float shortage.

At t: (1) settle orders due at or before t; (2) assess low balances/book exchanges
in random kiosk order; (3) serve exogenous customer requests; (4) check accounting,
record shortages/closures, update visuals and advance time. A duration d booked
at t settles at t+d before that demand batch, affecting d batches when closed.
All controls require Setup after changes; Go only reads the live balance-label switch.

## NETWORKS AND PORTABILITY

Synthetic random uses data-free undirected Erdos-Renyi graphs. Observed mutual
willingness uses private data/ CSVs with ties requiring mutual stated willingness,
not acquaintance or actual trades. Retain supplied isolates. Imported synthetic
uses synthetic-networks/ (pooled primary and corrected_hub sensitivity graphs,
20 draws per size 2–24 per generator). Both imported sources use the desktop
Choose local network button. Missing/invalid imports never silently become random.

GitHub synthetic: Load GitHub library, wait for Ready: 920 GitHub graphs, enter
ui-github-network-id (e.g. pooled-n20-r01), then Setup. URL changes require reload;
replace main with a commit SHA for reproducibility. All three CSVs must validate
before use. Cancel invalidates partial/stale responses. Missing replies expire
after 30 seconds when the monitor, Setup or Go checks status. Web HTTP failures
may also show the standard Web dialog. Use random networks returns to data-free
operation. Random/local modes make no HTTP requests. Desktop/headless needs
python scripts/install_netlogo_fetch.py once (official Fetch 1.0.5, Java 11).
Web supplies Fetch. Code-owned globals and ui- widgets remain separate.

## OUTPUTS AND LIMITS

Service metrics separate deposits and withdrawals. Peer trade counts are separate
from external counts; the Pending trades monitor counts both, while harness
pending_trades remains peer-only with external_pending alongside it. Fees and
subsidies are excluded from gross settled liquidity value and tracked separately.
The fee plot reconciles requester payments + subsidy = supplier fee income.
Mean peer grid distance concerns initiated trades; pending subsidy is separate
from subsidy spent. Agent-level earned-commissions and paid-commissions record
distributional outcomes. Reservations include cash fees owed by e-float buyers.

Gray squares show closed shops, purple shows other external-pending kiosks, and
amber arrows show peer commitments. The closure plot is the mean closed kiosks
per completed batch. The grid and spring layout are model assumptions, not map data.
The model has no operating expenses, endogenous participation, credit, repayment,
default, bank congestion, reputation, learning or changing network ties. It tests
conditional mechanisms rather than exact Tanzanian policy effects. V1 remains
the unchanged abstract benchmark; v2's spatial matching intentionally differs
even with zero commissions. See experiments/VERSIONS.md for controls, experiments
and accounting. Real surveys, exports and fitted objects remain private/ignored.
]]></info>
